Targets: common
Releases: 9.1.0-9.1.2
Subject: Backport: fix the crash-safety bug of newly created or reset sequences
Purpose: If a crash occurred right after the first nextval() of a new sequence, WAL replay restored the sequence to a state in which that nextval() had not happened, so the first value was handed out a second time (a duplicate key for a serial column). Sequence creation wrote a WAL record that claimed is_called = true while the stored tuple said log_cnt = 1, and ALTER SEQUENCE (which every serial column runs once) wrote a record with the real state. The fix logs the real state, makes nextval() log at the first call, resets log_cnt where ALTER SEQUENCE changes anything that affects future values, and logs the tuple itself (t_data, t_len) instead of the page contents. This build is a browser database that can be killed at any time, and the offline-use test of the demo hit exactly this duplicate key. PostgreSQL has the fix from 9.0.9.
Components: src/backend/commands/sequence.c
Checks: tests/browser/demo.spec.ts "the app is installed for offline use, then works with the server stopped and the network off": the demo reloads the page right after its first note and must not see a duplicate key
Origin: borrowed
Source-URL: https://github.com/postgres/postgres/commit/aa7cd144062bef3baf8fd4548c0f54bb108c3270
Source-Commit: aa7cd144062bef3baf8fd4548c0f54bb108c3270
Source-License: PostgreSQL License
Adaptation: The commit of the 9.1 branch (REL9_1_STABLE, Tom Lane, 2012-07-25, bug #6748), reduced to sequence.c and applied to the 9.1.x releases before the local page copy in sequence redo (9.1.3), which have the xmax cleanup in read_seq_tuple() that the commit builds on; the one line of seq_redo() next to the page copy is adapted to the code without it. It is the 9.1 counterpart of the 9.0 variants of this patch (the 9.0 branch has its own commit).

